WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:09.170
There are 168 different currencies in the
world and billions of dollars worth on new

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:12.570
banknotes are printed every day across the
world.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:19.460
Here's some of the most amazing facts about
money.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:23.710
1.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:30.949
Before coins were invented in 1500 BC, people
used to trade in all kinds of strange commodities,

00:00:30.949 --> 00:00:40.510
such as gold, rice, fish, barley, salt, tea
leaves, cows, blood, and even bat guano.

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:46.450
In America the word "buck" to mean money comes
from the days when people used to trade various

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:50.670
animal skins, including buckskins as currency.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:51.670
2.

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:56.070
The art of making money is precise and complex.

00:00:56.070 --> 00:01:01.530
Each coin has to be exactly the same as the
next, so that people accept that they are

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:04.600
worth the same amount of money.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:10.700
Traditionally coins were made by either striking
metal between two engraved dies, or by casting

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:12.880
them in moulds.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:19.590
However in the 18th century coins were made
by new steam powered machinery, which greatly

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:23.330
increased the number of coins that could be
produced in a day.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:24.330
3.

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:29.110
The production of bank notes however is a
bit money complicated.

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:35.049
Originally bank notes were black and white
but now almost all of them are brightly coloured

00:01:35.049 --> 00:01:41.850
and feature highly complex designs, which
makes them harder to counterfeit but also

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:45.210
instantly recognisable as a national currency.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:46.210
4.

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:54.070
In 1923, hyperinflation caused German currency
to lose almost all of its value.

00:01:54.070 --> 00:02:00.189
It became so worthless that people gave it
to children to play with or even used it as

00:02:00.189 --> 00:02:01.189
wallpaper.

00:02:01.189 --> 00:02:02.189
5.

00:02:02.189 --> 00:02:05.710
India has a banknote of zero value.

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:12.239
They look exactly the same as other banknotes
but are worth exactly zero rupees.

00:02:12.239 --> 00:02:15.370
You might ask, what's the point?

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:20.070
Corruption in India is widespread and a major
concern.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:25.840
Many government officials in India require
monetary bribes for all sorts of permits and

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:26.900
services.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:33.599
The zero rupee banknote serves an important
purpose, it is given to officials who request

00:02:33.599 --> 00:02:38.840
bribes as a statement of peaceful non-cooperation.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:47.000
Each zero rupee note bares a declaration that
states "I promise to neither accept nor give

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.540
a bribe".

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:52.110
It's sole purpose is to make a statement.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:59.500
Ironically this makes the zero rupee banknote
both valuable and worth absolutely nothing

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:00.920
at the same time.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:01.920
6.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:08.950
In most countries and currencies one pence
pieces cost more money to make than they are

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:10.450
actually worth.

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:19.670
The US treasury spends 2.41 cents to mint
each penny, making a huge loss.

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:25.160
Because of this the Canadian government stopped
producing pennies in 2013.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:26.260
7.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:30.900
The world's largest counterfeiter of US currency
is North Korea.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:39.099
They produce high quality fakes that the American
Secret Service have nicknamed "superdollars"

00:03:39.099 --> 00:03:43.610
because they so closely resemble genuine notes.

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:44.610
8.

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:51.440
The production of banknotes requires zero
trees, no paper is used at all.

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:59.069
All paper currency is made from a custom blend
of cotton and linen, usually around 25% cotton

00:03:59.069 --> 00:04:01.120
and 75% linen.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:02.120
9.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:07.080
90% of US banknotes contain traces of cocaine.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:08.100
10.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:14.330
Queen Elizabeth II is the queen of currency,
her portrait has graced the currencies of

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:21.769
33 different countries, more than any other
individual in history.

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:27.650
Canada was the first country to use the British
monarch's portrait on their banknotes in 1935,

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:35.419
when it printed the, at the time, 9-year-old
princess on its $20 notes.

00:04:35.419 --> 00:04:36.419
11.

00:04:36.419 --> 00:04:40.650
More monopoly money is printed every year
than real money.

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:41.650
12.

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:50.560
If you have just $10 in your pocket and no
debts, you are wealthier than 25% of Americans.

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:51.560
13.

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:58.050
A cheque is only an instruction to a bank,
it therefore, can be written on absolutely

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:05.180
anything, and as long as it has all the necessary
components, it will be honoured by the bank.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:10.889
Cheques have been written on doors, stone
slabs, bananas and even cows.

00:05:10.889 --> 00:05:12.150
14.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:14.139
Banknotes are pretty gross.

00:05:14.139 --> 00:05:23.370
A study showed that 14% of banknotes contain
fecal matter and 94% contain pathogens.

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:30.380
Viruses and bacteria can live on most surfaces
for about 48 hours, but they can live on paper

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:33.610
money for up to 17 days.

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:34.639
15.

00:05:34.639 --> 00:05:39.889
Icelanders pay for things using credit and
debit cards more than any other country in

00:05:39.889 --> 00:05:40.889
the world.

00:05:40.889 --> 00:05:49.889
70% of all business in Iceland is done with
plastic, compared to 39% in North America.

00:05:49.889 --> 00:05:50.889
16.

00:05:50.889 --> 00:05:56.389
Australia makes its banknotes from Polymer,
which is a type of synthetic plastic.

00:05:56.389 --> 00:06:04.730
They last for 2.5 times longer than traditional
cotton and linen banknotes and are surprisingly

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.419
more eco friendly.

00:06:06.419 --> 00:06:09.479
They are also harder to counterfeit.

00:06:09.479 --> 00:06:14.770
The UK will also be switching to plastic banknotes
in 2016.

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:15.770
17.

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:19.750
Some coins have grooved ridges around their
edges.

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:24.990
They serve little purpose today, but they
were originally added in the days when coins

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:29.889
were made from precious metals such as silver
and gold.

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:35.020
People used to scrape the edges off the coins
before spending them, then sell the precious

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:36.789
metals they scraped off.

00:06:36.789 --> 00:06:41.250
The grooved ridges were added to stop people
doing this.

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:42.250
18.

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:48.479
A vast majority of the money in the world
is not physical money, it is simply numbers

00:06:48.479 --> 00:06:51.069
in the computers of banks.

00:06:51.069 --> 00:06:52.069
19.

00:06:52.069 --> 00:06:58.530
Money which cannot be backed by a physical
commodity such as gold is called Fiat money.

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:07.260
Fiat money includes digital funds, paper banknotes
and coins, these kinds of money are only valuable,

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:12.259
simply because a government declares it an
official currency.

00:07:12.259 --> 00:07:18.670
However other non-government backed fiat money
can become valuable, just because there's

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:26.330
a high demand for it, such as the emerging
Bitcoin currency, which has no centralised

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:28.000
control body.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:34.410
The only thing that makes Bitcoin and other
cryptocurrencies valuable is the simple fact

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:38.150
that lots of people want it.

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:39.150
20.

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:45.860
And finally, if all the gold in the world
were melted down into a single square block,

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:58.850
it would only be 21 metres on each side.

